window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["advanced-rag"]=[{"id":"advanced-rag-f0001","card":"q-advanced-rag-001-overview","sec":"advanced-rag-s001","q":"What Python version and environment setup does the Advanced RAG course use?","a":"<p>Python 3.11, with one clean virtual environment per chapter, each with its own requirements file.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Bash</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>python3.11 -m venv venv\nsource venv/bin/activate\npip install -r requirements.txt</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0002","card":"q-advanced-rag-002-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"What is a vector database, and why is \"database\" a misnomer?","a":"<p>It works with unstructured data as vectors. It is really a <strong>compute engine for vector data</strong>. Embeddings are long lists of numbers, hundreds to thousands of them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0003","card":"q-advanced-rag-002-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"Why are embeddings called \"embeddings\"?","a":"<p>They come from the second-to-last layer of a deep neural network. That layer holds what the network learned, before the predictive layer is applied.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0004","card":"q-advanced-rag-002-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"What does this code print for the embedding dimensions, and which model is used?","a":"<p>It uses the <code>all-MiniLM-L6-v2</code> Sentence Transformers model and prints 384 dimensions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model = SentenceTransformer(&quot;all-MiniLM-L6-v2&quot;)\nembedding = model.encode(text)\nprint(f&quot;Embedding dimensions: {len(embedding)}&quot;)  # 384</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0005","card":"q-advanced-rag-002-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"What are embedding models?","a":"<p>Deep neural networks that turn data into vectors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0006","card":"q-advanced-rag-002-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"What are the two rules for using embedding models in RAG, and why does each exist?","a":"<ul>\n  <li><strong>Match data type to model type.</strong> Image models for images, text models for text. Even multimodal models need the input type specified.</li>\n  <li><strong>Use the same model for indexing and retrieval.</strong> Only same-size vectors can be compared, so the query must use the model that vectorized the data.</li>\n</ul>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0007","card":"q-advanced-rag-002-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"Why is <code>text_model.encode(image)</code> wrong, and why is <code>different_model.encode(\"search query\")</code> wrong?","a":"<p>A text model cannot embed images. A different model than the one used for indexing gives different dimensions, so the vectors cannot be compared.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>text_model = SentenceTransformer(&quot;all-MiniLM-L6-v2&quot;)  # text only\n# text_model.encode(image)  # WRONG: text model can&#x27;t embed images\nquery_embedding = text_model.encode(&quot;search query&quot;)     # same model as indexing\n# different_model.encode(&quot;search query&quot;)  # WRONG: different dimensions</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0008","card":"q-advanced-rag-003-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"What role do LLMs play in RAG, and what gap does RAG fill?","a":"<p>The LLM is the interface to your data. Most LLMs, including GPT-4, are transformers that predict the next token. They are trained on public data and have no access to yours. RAG fills that gap.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0009","card":"q-advanced-rag-003-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"What are the steps of the RAG pipeline?","a":"<p>Question, then embedding model, then vector DB similarity search, then relevant context, then the LLM (question plus context), then the answer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0010","card":"q-advanced-rag-003-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"In the RAG pipeline code, how is the question vectorized?","a":"<p>With the same Sentence Transformers model, converted to a list.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>model = SentenceTransformer(&quot;all-MiniLM-L6-v2&quot;)\nquery = &quot;What is retrieval augmented generation?&quot;\nquery_embedding = model.encode(query).tolist()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0011","card":"q-advanced-rag-003-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"In the RAG pipeline code, how is the vector database searched for similar embeddings?","a":"<p>By passing the query vector to the vector DB search with a limit. The call is pseudo-code and depends on your vector DB.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>results = vector_db.search(\n    collection=&quot;documents&quot;,\n    query_vector=query_embedding,\n    limit=5\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0012","card":"q-advanced-rag-003-1-core-concepts-and-rag-pipeline","sec":"advanced-rag-s002","q":"In the RAG pipeline code, how is the retrieved context built and passed to the LLM together with the question?","a":"<p>Join the retrieved texts into one context string, then send it with the question in the user message of a chat completion call.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>context = &quot;\\n&quot;.join([r[&quot;text&quot;] for r in results])\nresponse = openai.chat.completions.create(\n    model=&quot;gpt-4&quot;,\n    messages=[\n        {&quot;role&quot;: &quot;system&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: &quot;Answer based on the provided context.&quot;},\n        {&quot;role&quot;: &quot;user&quot;, &quot;content&quot;: f&quot;Context:\\n{context}\\n\\nQuestion: {query}&quot;}\n    ]\n)\nprint(response.choices[0].message.content)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0013","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"What are the three components of RAG data preprocessing?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>Chunking</strong></td><td>Breaking big text into smaller blocks</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Embeddings</strong></td><td>Vectors from embedding models; semantic meaning in quantifiable form</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Metadata</strong></td><td>Data stored alongside embeddings; critical for customizable RAG apps</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0014","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"Why do you chunk text for RAG?","a":"<p>Humans read one coherent sentence or paragraph at a time, and LLMs work the same way.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0015","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"What is the context window, and is it the ideal amount to fill?","a":"<p>It is the maximum number of tokens an LLM handles at once (GPT-4 Turbo is about 128k). It is a cap, not the ideal amount.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0016","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"Dense vs sparse embeddings: how do they differ and which does RAG mostly use?","a":"<p>Dense embeddings have few zero values and are created by ML models. Sparse embeddings have many (more than 50%) zero values and are created by algorithms such as TF-IDF and BM25. RAG mostly uses dense embeddings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0017","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"What does this code show about a dense embedding?","a":"<p>It counts the zero values. A dense embedding has very few zeros (384 dimensions here).</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>dense_embedding = model.encode(&quot;Retrieval augmented generation improves LLM accuracy&quot;)\nzeros = np.count_nonzero(dense_embedding == 0)\nprint(f&quot;Dimensions: {len(dense_embedding)}&quot;)           # 384\nprint(f&quot;Zero values: {zeros}/{len(dense_embedding)}&quot;)  # very few zeros</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0018","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"Why must metadata include the original text?","a":"<p>Vector DBs retrieve by similarity, and an LLM cannot turn embeddings back into text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0019","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"What is the main use of metadata?","a":"<p>Filtering searches before similarity comparison.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0020","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"What are the two types of metadata in RAG preprocessing, with examples?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Processing metadata</td><td>Section title, paragraph number, chunk location</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Data metadata</td><td>Embedded text itself, publication date, author</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0021","card":"q-advanced-rag-004-2-preprocessing-chunking-embeddings-meta","sec":"advanced-rag-s003","q":"What does a RAG data point look like in code?","a":"<p>A dict with an <code>id</code>, the <code>text</code>, its <code>embedding</code> and a <code>metadata</code> dict.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>data_point = {\n    &quot;id&quot;: &quot;doc1_chunk3&quot;,\n    &quot;text&quot;: &quot;RAG combines retrieval with generation for accurate answers.&quot;,\n    &quot;embedding&quot;: model.encode(&quot;RAG combines retrieval with generation for accurate answers.&quot;).tolist(),\n    &quot;metadata&quot;: {\n        &quot;source&quot;: &quot;rag_handbook.pdf&quot;,\n        &quot;author&quot;: &quot;Yujian Tang&quot;,\n        &quot;page&quot;: 12,\n        &quot;section&quot;: &quot;Introduction&quot;,\n        &quot;chunk_index&quot;: 3,\n        &quot;publication_date&quot;: &quot;2024-01-15&quot;\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0022","card":"q-advanced-rag-005-chunking-considerations-the-three-c-s","sec":"advanced-rag-s004","q":"What are the three C's of chunking?","a":"<p>A chunk should be <strong>Consumable</strong>, <strong>Coherent</strong> and <strong>Contextual</strong>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0023","card":"q-advanced-rag-005-chunking-considerations-the-three-c-s","sec":"advanced-rag-s004","q":"What does \"Consumable\" mean for a chunk, technically and in common sense?","a":"<p>Technically, it fits the embedding model's context window, and at least 3 chunks fit in the LLM's context window (depends on top-K). In common sense terms, it is readable in one go.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0024","card":"q-advanced-rag-005-chunking-considerations-the-three-c-s","sec":"advanced-rag-s004","q":"What does \"Coherent\" mean for a chunk, and what is an example?","a":"<p>It does not start or stop mid-word, clause or sentence, and makes sense on its own. \"Curiosity killed the cat\" is coherent. \"killed the\" is not.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0025","card":"q-advanced-rag-005-chunking-considerations-the-three-c-s","sec":"advanced-rag-s004","q":"What does \"Contextual\" mean for a chunk, and what example shows a chunk that lacks it?","a":"<p>The chunk holds all the context needed to answer a question. \"Curiosity killed the cat\" is coherent but out of context. The full saying is \"...but satisfaction brought it back\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0026","card":"q-advanced-rag-006-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"What are the three chunking parameters?","a":"<p>Chunk size, chunk overlap and special characters (separators).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0027","card":"q-advanced-rag-006-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"What is chunk size, what is a good starting value, and is it a hard limit?","a":"<p>The number of characters per chunk. It depends on data structure. Start at about 100 words, or about 500 characters (a paragraph). Depending on the method it may be a hard limit or a guideline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0028","card":"q-advanced-rag-006-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"What is chunk overlap and what does it do?","a":"<p>The characters repeated between consecutive chunks. It carries context across chunks, such as the last sentence of the previous chunk.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0029","card":"q-advanced-rag-006-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"What are special characters (separators) in chunking?","a":"<p>The characters to split on, such as periods and newlines. They relax size limits so chunks end at natural boundaries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0030","card":"q-advanced-rag-006-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"How do you set chunk size, overlap and separators in <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code>?","a":"<p>Pass <code>chunk_size</code>, <code>chunk_overlap</code> and <code>separators</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=200,\n    chunk_overlap=50,\n    separators=[&quot;\\n\\n&quot;, &quot;\\n&quot;, &quot;. &quot;, &quot; &quot;, &quot;&quot;]\n)\nchunks = splitter.split_text(document)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0031","card":"q-advanced-rag-006-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"How does the separators list work in <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code>?","a":"<p>It is a priority order. The splitter tries paragraphs (double newlines) first, then single newlines, then sentence boundaries, then word boundaries, and character-level split as a last resort.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>separators = [\n    &quot;\\n\\n&quot;,  # First try: split on double newlines (paragraphs)\n    &quot;\\n&quot;,    # Then: single newlines\n    &quot;. &quot;,    # Then: sentence boundaries\n    &quot; &quot;,     # Then: word boundaries\n    &quot;&quot;       # Last resort: character-level split\n]</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0032","card":"q-advanced-rag-006-three-chunking-parameters","sec":"advanced-rag-s005","q":"What happens if a chunk exceeds <code>chunk_size</code> without hitting a separator?","a":"<p>The splitter relaxes the size limit and continues to the next separator. The result is more coherent chunks that are slightly larger. For example, with <code>chunk_size=500</code> and a 520-character paragraph, it keeps the full paragraph instead of cutting at exactly 500 characters mid-sentence.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0033","card":"q-advanced-rag-007-3-1-document-data-papers-reports-docs-bl","sec":"advanced-rag-s006","q":"What are the traits of document data (papers, reports, docs, blogs, news, API docs) for chunking?","a":"<p>Regularly sized blocks with little size variation. Size and overlap are easy to choose, and you usually split on <code>\\n\\n</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0034","card":"q-advanced-rag-007-3-1-document-data-papers-reports-docs-bl","sec":"advanced-rag-s006","q":"How do you configure a splitter for document data?","a":"<p>Use <code>RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter</code> with a size around 500, a small overlap and the paragraph separator first.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>doc_splitter = RecursiveCharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=500,\n    chunk_overlap=50,\n    separators=[&quot;\\n\\n&quot;, &quot;\\n&quot;, &quot;. &quot;, &quot; &quot;]\n)\nchunks = doc_splitter.split_text(paper)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0035","card":"q-advanced-rag-008-3-2-q-a-transcripts-podcasts-lectures-am","sec":"advanced-rag-s007","q":"What are the traits of Q&amp;A transcripts (podcasts, lectures, AMAs, talks) for chunking?","a":"<p>Short questions and longer answers that are semantically linked, since an answer is meaningless without its question.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0036","card":"q-advanced-rag-008-3-2-q-a-transcripts-podcasts-lectures-am","sec":"advanced-rag-s007","q":"How do you chunk Q&amp;A transcripts so question and answer stay connected?","a":"<p>Keep each pair coupled via metadata, but put them in separate chunks. Each chunk stores a <code>pair_id</code> that links the question to its answer.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for item in qa_transcript:\n    data_point = {\n        &quot;text&quot;: item[&quot;text&quot;],\n        &quot;metadata&quot;: {\n            &quot;role&quot;: item[&quot;role&quot;],\n            &quot;pair_id&quot;: item[&quot;pair_id&quot;],  # links Q to A\n        }\n    }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0037","card":"q-advanced-rag-009-3-3-chat-transcripts-support-chats-texts","sec":"advanced-rag-s008","q":"What makes chat transcripts (support chats, texts, DMs, group chats) hard to chunk?","a":"<p>Chunk sizes are irregular (a word to a paragraph), and linking is unpredictable because the same person sends several messages in a row.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0038","card":"q-advanced-rag-009-3-3-chat-transcripts-support-chats-texts","sec":"advanced-rag-s008","q":"What matters most when chunking chat transcripts?","a":"<p>Separators that identify the sender are critical. Metadata tracks the sender and the message order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0039","card":"q-advanced-rag-009-3-3-chat-transcripts-support-chats-texts","sec":"advanced-rag-s008","q":"What metadata does each chat message chunk carry?","a":"<p>The sender, timestamp, message index and conversation id.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for i, msg in enumerate(chat_messages):\n    data_point = {\n        &quot;text&quot;: msg[&quot;text&quot;],\n        &quot;metadata&quot;: {\n            &quot;sender&quot;: msg[&quot;sender&quot;],\n            &quot;timestamp&quot;: msg[&quot;timestamp&quot;],\n            &quot;message_index&quot;: i,\n            &quot;conversation_id&quot;: &quot;conv_001&quot;,\n        }\n    }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0040","card":"q-advanced-rag-010-4-embeddings-model-choice-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s009","q":"What are the three decisions about embeddings?","a":"<p>Which model, what to embed, and how to compare.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0041","card":"q-advanced-rag-010-4-embeddings-model-choice-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s009","q":"Which example embedding model fits each data type: images, text, audio?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Data type</th><th>Example model</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Images</td><td>ResNet50</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Text</td><td>Sentence Transformers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Audio</td><td>Whisper</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0042","card":"q-advanced-rag-010-4-embeddings-model-choice-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s009","q":"Where do you check which text embedding models rank best?","a":"<p>The Hugging Face MTEB leaderboard. Specialized data (such as CSV) may need its own model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0043","card":"q-advanced-rag-010-4-embeddings-model-choice-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s009","q":"Are embedding models always LLMs?","a":"<p>No. LLMs can produce embeddings, but they are different things.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0044","card":"q-advanced-rag-011-picking-a-model","sec":"advanced-rag-s010","q":"What factors decide which embedding model to pick?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Embedding size</td><td>Dimensionality (384, 768, 1536). Larger means more compute to compare. Only same-size embeddings are comparable.</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Model size</td><td>Smaller is cheaper; larger is more fine-grained</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Training data</td><td>Language, structure (chat vs essays), data size</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0045","card":"q-advanced-rag-011-picking-a-model","sec":"advanced-rag-s010","q":"Why would a Chinese embedding model not work for Arabic text?","a":"<p>Training data matters. The model must be trained on the language of your data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0046","card":"q-advanced-rag-011-picking-a-model","sec":"advanced-rag-s010","q":"What are the dimensions of <code>all-MiniLM-L6-v2</code> and <code>all-mpnet-base-v2</code>, and can you compare their vectors?","a":"<p><code>all-MiniLM-L6-v2</code> gives 384 dimensions and is fast. <code>all-mpnet-base-v2</code> gives 768 and is more fine-grained. You cannot compare vectors from the two because the sizes differ.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>small_model = SentenceTransformer(&quot;all-MiniLM-L6-v2&quot;)   # 384 dims, fast\nlarge_model = SentenceTransformer(&quot;all-mpnet-base-v2&quot;)  # 768 dims, more fine-grained\n# CANNOT compare these: different sizes\n# cosine_similarity(small_emb, large_emb)  # ERROR</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0047","card":"q-advanced-rag-011-picking-a-model","sec":"advanced-rag-s010","q":"Which algorithmic (non-neural) models produce sparse or binary embeddings?","a":"<p><strong>TF-IDF</strong> (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency), <strong>SPLADE</strong> (Sparse Lexical and Expansion Models) and <strong>BM25</strong> (Best Matching 25).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0048","card":"q-advanced-rag-012-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"What are the three strategies for what to embed, and what is stored in metadata for each?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Embedded</th><th>Stored in metadata</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Embed the chunk</td><td>The chunk text</td><td>Standard metadata</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Small-to-big</strong></td><td>Individual sentences</td><td>Large paragraph</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Big-to-small</strong></td><td>Large paragraphs</td><td>Individual sentences</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0049","card":"q-advanced-rag-012-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"Is embedding the chunk as-is enough for production RAG?","a":"<p>No. It is fine for basic tasks only. Small-to-big and big-to-small improve retrieval even when chunks are already good.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>chunk = &quot;RAG combines retrieval with generation for grounded responses.&quot;\nembedding = model.encode(chunk).tolist()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0050","card":"q-advanced-rag-012-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"What is small-to-big embedding, and when do you use it?","a":"<p>Embed a sentence and store the whole paragraph. Use it when sentences are very short. Retrieving the paragraph adds context and coherence.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0051","card":"q-advanced-rag-012-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"How does small-to-big look in code?","a":"<p>Embed each sentence, and keep the sentence and the full paragraph in metadata.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for sentence in sentences:\n    data_point = {\n        &quot;embedding&quot;: model.encode(sentence).tolist(),  # embed the sentence\n        &quot;metadata&quot;: {\n            &quot;sentence&quot;: sentence,\n            &quot;full_paragraph&quot;: paragraph,  # store the whole paragraph\n        }\n    }</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0052","card":"q-advanced-rag-012-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"What is big-to-small embedding, and when do you use it?","a":"<p>Embed the paragraph and store its sentences. Use it when sentences alone don't make sense (for example a period after \"Mr.\" breaks sentence splitting). Retrieving sentences separately allows post-processing before the LLM.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0053","card":"q-advanced-rag-012-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"How does big-to-small look in code?","a":"<p>Embed the whole paragraph, and keep the full text and the list of sentences in metadata.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>data_point = {\n    &quot;embedding&quot;: model.encode(paragraph).tolist(),  # embed the paragraph\n    &quot;metadata&quot;: {\n        &quot;full_text&quot;: paragraph,\n        &quot;sentences&quot;: sentences,  # store individual sentences\n    }\n}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0054","card":"q-advanced-rag-012-what-to-embed","sec":"advanced-rag-s011","q":"What does non-English data need in a RAG system, and what are the options?","a":"<p>A model trained on that language. A multilingual LLM (GPT-3.5+, Mixtral, Qwen) is easy but less compute-friendly. A language-specific model (search MTEB for French, Polish, Chinese and so on) is more efficient and gives better results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0055","card":"q-advanced-rag-013-distance-metrics-for-dense-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s012","q":"What are the three distance metrics for dense vectors, with their formulas?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Measures</th><th>Formula</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>Euclidean</strong></td><td>Spatial distance</td><td>√(Σ(aᵢ − bᵢ)²)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Cosine similarity</strong></td><td>Orientation difference</td><td>(A·B) / (‖A‖ × ‖B‖)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Inner (dot) product</strong></td><td>Projection of one vector on another</td><td>Σ(aᵢ × bᵢ)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0056","card":"q-advanced-rag-013-distance-metrics-for-dense-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s012","q":"What is the intuition for Euclidean distance?","a":"<p>The straight-line distance between two points.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0057","card":"q-advanced-rag-013-distance-metrics-for-dense-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s012","q":"What is the intuition for cosine similarity, and what is its cost?","a":"<p>It is the angle between two arrows from the origin. It is the most computationally expensive of the three.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0058","card":"q-advanced-rag-013-distance-metrics-for-dense-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s012","q":"How does inner (dot) product relate to cosine similarity?","a":"<p>It is cosine without dividing by magnitudes. It is the simplest of the three.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0059","card":"q-advanced-rag-013-distance-metrics-for-dense-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s012","q":"How do you compute Euclidean, cosine and dot product in NumPy?","a":"<p>Use these three lines. For <code>a = [1, 2, 3]</code> and <code>b = [4, 5, 6]</code> the results are 5.1962, 0.9746 and 32.0.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>euclidean = np.sqrt(np.sum((a - b) ** 2))\ncosine = np.dot(a, b) / (np.linalg.norm(a) * np.linalg.norm(b))\ndot_product = np.dot(a, b)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0060","card":"q-advanced-rag-014-distance-metrics-for-sparse-binary-vecto","sec":"advanced-rag-s013","q":"What does Hamming distance measure, and how is it computed?","a":"<p>The number of positions where two binary vectors differ. Take the XOR and count the 1s.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0061","card":"q-advanced-rag-014-distance-metrics-for-sparse-binary-vecto","sec":"advanced-rag-s013","q":"What is Jaccard distance?","a":"<p>1 minus the Jaccard similarity: (A∪B − A∩B) / A∪B.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0062","card":"q-advanced-rag-014-distance-metrics-for-sparse-binary-vecto","sec":"advanced-rag-s013","q":"How do you compute Hamming and Jaccard distance in NumPy?","a":"<p>Hamming is the sum of the XOR. Jaccard is 1 minus intersection over union. For the example vectors the results are 3 and 0.6.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>hamming = np.sum(a ^ b)\nintersection = np.sum(a &amp; b)  # both are 1\nunion = np.sum(a | b)         # either is 1\njaccard_distance = 1 - (intersection / union)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0063","card":"q-advanced-rag-015-5-metadata","sec":"advanced-rag-s014","q":"Why does metadata matter in a vector database?","a":"<p>Without metadata you are just comparing numbers. Metadata is what makes vector DBs useful.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0064","card":"q-advanced-rag-015-5-metadata","sec":"advanced-rag-s014","q":"What are chunking metadata and non-chunking metadata, and what is each used for?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Source</th><th>Examples</th><th>Main use</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>Chunking metadata</strong></td><td>Produced during chunking</td><td>Sentence number, subtitle, section header</td><td>Context and filtering (chunks from one section)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Non-chunking metadata</strong></td><td>External to chunking</td><td>Author, last-updated date, document title</td><td>Filtering (written by you, updated last month)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0065","card":"q-advanced-rag-015-5-metadata","sec":"advanced-rag-s014","q":"What are the two options for storing metadata, and which is more popular for RAG?","a":"<p>(a) Link to an external relational DB, or (b) store it directly in the vector store. Option (b) is more popular for RAG because filtering is easier and faster.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0066","card":"q-advanced-rag-016-chunk","sec":"advanced-rag-s015","q":"In the LangChain + FAISS text RAG demo, how do you chunk each file into 128-character chunks with 32 characters of overlap?","a":"<p>Read each file and split it with <code>CharacterTextSplitter</code>, then wrap each chunk in a <code>Document</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>splitter = CharacterTextSplitter(chunk_size=128, chunk_overlap=32)\nchunks = splitter.split_text(text)\nfor chunk in chunks:\n    all_docs.append(Document(page_content=chunk))</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0067","card":"q-advanced-rag-016-chunk","sec":"advanced-rag-s015","q":"Does <code>CharacterTextSplitter</code> treat <code>chunk_size</code> as a hard limit?","a":"<p>No. It treats chunk size as a guideline, so some chunks exceed 128 characters. This is fixed in the oversized chunks challenge with a custom separator.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0068","card":"q-advanced-rag-017-add-metadata","sec":"advanced-rag-s016","q":"How do you add a document title and chunk number to each chunk in the text RAG demo?","a":"<p>Use <code>enumerate</code> for the chunk number and pass a <code>metadata</code> dict to each <code>Document</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>for i, chunk in enumerate(chunks):  # enumerate for chunk number\n    doc = Document(\n        page_content=chunk,\n        metadata={\n            &quot;doc_title&quot;: file,      # document title\n            &quot;chunk_number&quot;: i,      # where in the document\n        }\n    )\n    all_docs.append(doc)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0069","card":"q-advanced-rag-017-add-metadata","sec":"advanced-rag-s016","q":"What does the first document's metadata look like in the demo?","a":"<p><code>{'doc_title': 'binaryville.txt', 'chunk_number': 0}</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0070","card":"q-advanced-rag-018-embed-and-store","sec":"advanced-rag-s017","q":"What is FAISS?","a":"<p>Facebook AI Similarity Search, the library most AI-native vector DBs are built on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0071","card":"q-advanced-rag-018-embed-and-store","sec":"advanced-rag-s017","q":"Where does <code>HuggingFaceEmbeddings</code> live, and what is its default model?","a":"<p>It lives in <code>langchain_community</code>, because integrations were split out of core LangChain. The default model is <code>all-mpnet-base-v2</code>, which gives 768 dimensions. Only same-dimension vectors compare.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0072","card":"q-advanced-rag-018-embed-and-store","sec":"advanced-rag-s017","q":"How do you embed documents and store them in FAISS with LangChain?","a":"<p>Create <code>HuggingFaceEmbeddings</code> and call <code>FAISS.from_documents</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>embedding_function = HuggingFaceEmbeddings()  # default: all-mpnet-base-v2\nvector_store = FAISS.from_documents(\n    documents=all_docs,\n    embedding=embedding_function\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0073","card":"q-advanced-rag-019-query","sec":"advanced-rag-s018","q":"What does <code>as_retriever()</code> do in the LangChain FAISS demo?","a":"<p>It hides the string-to-embedding conversion and the query call. In RAG the LLM does the querying behind the scenes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0074","card":"q-advanced-rag-019-query","sec":"advanced-rag-s018","q":"What is the default top-K in LangChain FAISS?","a":"<p>4.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0075","card":"q-advanced-rag-019-query","sec":"advanced-rag-s018","q":"How do you retrieve the top documents for a question from the FAISS store?","a":"<p>Create a retriever with <code>as_retriever()</code> and call <code>invoke</code>. It returns the top 4 docs.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>retriever = vector_store.as_retriever()\nresults = retriever.invoke(&quot;What is Binaryville about?&quot;)  # top 4 docs</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0076","card":"q-advanced-rag-020-add-the-llm","sec":"advanced-rag-s019","q":"How do you build a prompt template with <code>{context}</code> and <code>{question}</code> placeholders in LangChain?","a":"<p>Write the template string and call <code>ChatPromptTemplate.from_template</code>. Both placeholders are filled at runtime.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>prompt_str = &quot;&quot;&quot;Answer the question based on the following context:\nContext: {context}\nQuestion: {question}\n&quot;&quot;&quot;\nprompt = ChatPromptTemplate.from_template(prompt_str)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0077","card":"q-advanced-rag-020-add-the-llm","sec":"advanced-rag-s019","q":"How is the full RAG chain assembled in LangChain?","a":"<p>A dict with the retriever and <code>RunnablePassthrough()</code> feeds the prompt, then the LLM, then the string parser.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>chain = (\n    {&quot;context&quot;: retriever, &quot;question&quot;: RunnablePassthrough()}\n    | prompt\n    | llm\n    | StrOutputParser()\n)\nresponse = chain.invoke(&quot;What is Binaryville about?&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0078","card":"q-advanced-rag-020-add-the-llm","sec":"advanced-rag-s019","q":"What does each step of the LangChain RAG chain do?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Component</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>1</td><td><code>retriever</code></td><td>Takes the question, queries the vector store, returns top-K context</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>2</td><td><code>RunnablePassthrough()</code></td><td>Passes the question string through unchanged</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>3</td><td><code>prompt</code></td><td>Fills <code>{context}</code> and <code>{question}</code></td></tr>\n      <tr><td>4</td><td><code>llm</code></td><td>Generates the response</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>5</td><td><code>StrOutputParser()</code></td><td>Parses output to a string</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0079","card":"q-advanced-rag-020-add-the-llm","sec":"advanced-rag-s019","q":"How does the demo load the OpenAI API key?","a":"<p>With <code>load_dotenv()</code>, which reads the key from a <code>.env</code> file. The LLM is then created with <code>OpenAI()</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>load_dotenv()  # API key from .env\nllm = OpenAI()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0080","card":"q-advanced-rag-021-challenge-1-cite-document-sources-prompt","sec":"advanced-rag-s020","q":"How can the RAG chain make the LLM cite which documents it used for the answer?","a":"<p>Document names are already in metadata (<code>doc_title</code>), and the retriever passes both <code>page_content</code> and <code>metadata</code> to the LLM. Add one sentence to the prompt asking for citations. The chain is rebuilt as before.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>prompt_str = &quot;&quot;&quot;Answer the question based on the following context.\nCite the document sources you found the answer in.\nContext: {context}\nQuestion: {question}\n&quot;&quot;&quot;</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0081","card":"q-advanced-rag-022-challenge-2-oversized-chunks-custom-sepa","sec":"advanced-rag-s021","q":"Why does <code>CharacterTextSplitter</code> produce oversized chunks with warnings, and what are its defaults?","a":"<p>The defaults are <code>separator=\"\\n\\n\"</code> and <code>is_separator_regex=False</code>. If the text has no double newline inside the size window, the splitter cannot split, so chunks exceed the size and warnings appear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0082","card":"q-advanced-rag-022-challenge-2-oversized-chunks-custom-sepa","sec":"advanced-rag-s021","q":"How do you fix oversized chunks from <code>CharacterTextSplitter</code>?","a":"<p>Use a single newline separator.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>splitter_after = CharacterTextSplitter(\n    chunk_size=128,\n    chunk_overlap=32,\n    separator=&quot;\\n&quot;  # split on single newlines instead\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0083","card":"q-advanced-rag-022-challenge-2-oversized-chunks-custom-sepa","sec":"advanced-rag-s021","q":"What does the separator mean in <code>CharacterTextSplitter</code>?","a":"<p>It is where the splitter may cut. <code>\"\\n\\n\"</code> waits for paragraph breaks. <code>\"\\n\"</code> can cut at any line break, which gives correctly sized chunks while keeping coherence at line boundaries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0084","card":"q-advanced-rag-023-two-types-of-image-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s022","q":"What are the two types of image vectors, and which one does RAG use?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Captures</th><th>Created by</th><th>Used in RAG?</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>Semantic embeddings</strong></td><td>The <em>meaning</em> of the image</td><td>Deep learning model's second-to-last layer</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>Visual / pixel embeddings</strong></td><td>What the image <em>literally looks like</em></td><td>Compressed image input vector (e.g. PyTorch model input)</td><td>Not typically</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0085","card":"q-advanced-rag-023-two-types-of-image-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s022","q":"Where does a semantic image embedding come from?","a":"<p>The image goes through the input layer and hidden layers to the output (prediction). The second-to-last layer holds the learned meaning, and that is the embedding.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0086","card":"q-advanced-rag-023-two-types-of-image-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s022","q":"What do pixel embeddings encode, compared with semantic embeddings?","a":"<p>They are also long number lists, but they encode raw pixels, not meaning. RAG uses semantic embeddings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0087","card":"q-advanced-rag-023-two-types-of-image-vectors","sec":"advanced-rag-s022","q":"How do you capture a ResNet50 semantic embedding with a forward hook?","a":"<p>Load a pretrained ResNet50 in eval mode, register a hook on <code>avgpool</code> that saves the output, then run an image through it. The embedding has 2048 dimensions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>resnet = models.resnet50(pretrained=True)\nresnet.eval()\nsemantic_embeddings = {}\ndef hook_fn(module, input, output):\n    semantic_embeddings[&quot;embedding&quot;] = output.squeeze().detach().numpy()\nresnet.avgpool.register_forward_hook(hook_fn)\nwith torch.no_grad():\n    resnet(input_tensor)  # embedding captured by the hook</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0088","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"What are vision models?","a":"<p>Deep neural networks for computer vision tasks such as classification, segmentation and object detection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0089","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"How did vision model types evolve?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Era</th><th>Model type</th><th>Key innovation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>1960s</td><td>Simple neural networks</td><td>Fully connected layers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>1993</td><td><strong>CNNs</strong></td><td>Convolution + max pooling; dominated about 30 years</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>2019</td><td><strong>Vision Transformers (ViT)</strong></td><td>Attention mechanism applied to vision</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0090","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"What is a CNN made of?","a":"<p>A convolutional layer plus a pooling layer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0091","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"How does the convolution step in a CNN work?","a":"<p>Slide a <strong>learnable</strong> filter (for example 3×3) over regions of the image. Each output cell is the element-wise multiply of the window with the filter, summed. It extracts local context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0092","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"What does max pooling do?","a":"<p>It compresses the convolution output by taking the max of each region. For example max(12, 20, 8, 12) = 20.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0093","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"What do a convolution layer and max pooling layer do to a <code>(batch, 3, 224, 224)</code> input in PyTorch?","a":"<p>The conv layer gives <code>(batch, 16, 224, 224)</code>. The pool layer halves the spatial dimensions to <code>(batch, 16, 112, 112)</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>conv_layer = nn.Conv2d(in_channels=3, out_channels=16, kernel_size=3, padding=1)\npool_layer = nn.MaxPool2d(kernel_size=2, stride=2)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0094","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"What is a Vision Transformer (ViT)?","a":"<p>A model that takes attention from language transformers and applies it to images.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0095","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"What is the ViT pipeline?","a":"<p>Image, split into N×N <strong>patches</strong> (like words), patch embeddings, add a <strong>[CLS] token</strong> and <strong>positional encoding</strong>, transformer encoder (attention), then an MLP head (classification, segmentation and so on).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0096","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"What is the [CLS] token in ViT, and what is positional encoding?","a":"<p>The [CLS] token is a special token whose output represents the whole image, so it is the image embedding. Positional encoding tells the model where each patch sits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0097","card":"q-advanced-rag-024-vision-models-101","sec":"advanced-rag-s023","q":"How do you get a ViT image embedding with Hugging Face, and how many dimensions does it have?","a":"<p>Run the model and take the [CLS] token from the last hidden state. The embedding has 768 dimensions.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>feature_extractor = ViTFeatureExtractor.from_pretrained(&quot;google/vit-base-patch16-224&quot;)\nmodel = ViTModel.from_pretrained(&quot;google/vit-base-patch16-224&quot;)\n# [CLS] token embedding: semantic representation of the entire image\nimage_embedding = outputs.last_hidden_state[:, 0, :].squeeze().numpy()</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0098","card":"q-advanced-rag-025-get-semantic-vectors-openclip-via-langch","sec":"advanced-rag-s024","q":"What does <code>embed_image</code> take as input in LangChain's <code>OpenCLIPEmbeddings</code>?","a":"<p>A <strong>list of image paths or URIs</strong>, not a single image.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0099","card":"q-advanced-rag-025-get-semantic-vectors-openclip-via-langch","sec":"advanced-rag-s024","q":"How many dimensions do OpenCLIP embeddings have?","a":"<p>1024.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0100","card":"q-advanced-rag-025-get-semantic-vectors-openclip-via-langch","sec":"advanced-rag-s024","q":"How do you get OpenCLIP embeddings for a folder of images?","a":"<p>Create <code>OpenCLIPEmbeddings</code> and call <code>embed_image</code> on the list of paths.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>image_paths = glob.glob(&quot;images/*.jpg&quot;)\nclip_embeddings = OpenCLIPEmbeddings()\nembeddings = clip_embeddings.embed_image(image_paths)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0101","card":"q-advanced-rag-026-store-image-vectors-in-faiss","sec":"advanced-rag-s025","q":"How are images stored in FAISS for RAG, following the same pattern as text RAG?","a":"<p>Base64-encode each image as <code>page_content</code> and store the file path as metadata.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>doc = Document(\n    page_content=encoded,          # base64 encoded image\n    metadata={&quot;image_path&quot;: path}  # store the file path\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0102","card":"q-advanced-rag-026-store-image-vectors-in-faiss","sec":"advanced-rag-s025","q":"What does the <code>encode_image</code> helper do?","a":"<p>It reads the file bytes and returns a UTF-8 base64 string, for the LLM later. It is reused in every later image demo.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def encode_image(file_path: str) -&gt; str:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Read file bytes, return UTF-8 base64 string (for the LLM later).&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    with open(file_path, &quot;rb&quot;) as f:\n        return base64.b64encode(f.read()).decode(&quot;utf-8&quot;)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0103","card":"q-advanced-rag-026-store-image-vectors-in-faiss","sec":"advanced-rag-s025","q":"How do you build the FAISS store for image documents with OpenCLIP?","a":"<p>Pass the documents and <code>OpenCLIPEmbeddings</code> to <code>FAISS.from_documents</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>embedding_function = OpenCLIPEmbeddings()\nvector_store = FAISS.from_documents(\n    documents=all_docs,\n    embedding=embedding_function\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0104","card":"q-advanced-rag-027-compare-images-semantically","sec":"advanced-rag-s026","q":"How do you search for images similar to a query image?","a":"<p>Use the same <code>as_retriever()</code> and <code>invoke()</code> pattern, but pass the <strong>base64 string of an image</strong> instead of text. Results are ranked by semantic similarity.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>retriever = vector_store.as_retriever()\nquery_image = encode_image(&quot;images/cat1.jpg&quot;)\nresults = retriever.invoke(query_image)  # top 4 most similar images</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0105","card":"q-advanced-rag-027-compare-images-semantically","sec":"advanced-rag-s026","q":"What sanity check does image-to-image search give you?","a":"<p>The top hit should be the query image itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0106","card":"q-advanced-rag-028-challenge-which-dog-is-most-similar-to-t","sec":"advanced-rag-s027","q":"What is the approach to find which dog image is most similar to the cats?","a":"<p>For each dog image, retrieve the top 4 and count the cat images, weighted by rank. Rank i gets <code>4 - i</code> points (4, 3, 2, 1). A cat at rank 0 and another at rank 2 scores 4 + 2 = 6.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0107","card":"q-advanced-rag-028-challenge-which-dog-is-most-similar-to-t","sec":"advanced-rag-s027","q":"How is the rank-weighted cat score computed in code?","a":"<p>Loop over the results and add <code>4 - i</code> for each cat image.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>cats_retrieved = 0\nfor i, doc in enumerate(results):\n    source = doc.metadata[&quot;image_path&quot;]\n    if &quot;cat&quot; in source:\n        cats_retrieved += 4 - i  # inverse weight by rank\ndog_cat_scores[dog_path] = cats_retrieved</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0108","card":"q-advanced-rag-028-challenge-which-dog-is-most-similar-to-t","sec":"advanced-rag-s027","q":"In the demo, which dog image was most similar to the cats, and what were the scores?","a":"<p><code>images/dog1.jpg</code> with a cat score of 7. <code>images/dog2.jpg</code> scored 3 and <code>images/dog3.jpg</code> scored 0.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0109","card":"q-advanced-rag-029-10-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s028","q":"What does multimodal mean, and which modalities does AI mainly emulate?","a":"<p>Multiple data types. Humans are multimodal (sight, hearing and so on), and AI emulates mainly sight and hearing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0110","card":"q-advanced-rag-029-10-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s028","q":"Which combinations are widely agreed to be multimodal, and which are contested?","a":"<p>Widely agreed: images plus text, images plus audio, video (sight plus sound). Contested, because all are \"sight\" structured differently: PDFs plus CSVs, text plus tables, tables plus graphs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0111","card":"q-advanced-rag-029-10-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s028","q":"Why do contested combinations still count as multimodal?","a":"<p>Each type needs different ML models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0112","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"What are the three ways to do multimodal RAG?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Models</th><th>Vector stores</th><th>Complexity</th><th>Usage</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>1. Single multimodal model</strong> (canonical)</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>Low</td><td>Most common</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>2. Multiple models</strong> (one per data type)</td><td>N</td><td>N</td><td>High</td><td>Rare, manual</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>3. Multiple multimodal models</strong></td><td>N</td><td>N</td><td>Very high</td><td>Almost never</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0113","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"How does method 1 (single multimodal model) work?","a":"<p>One model (for example CLIP for images and text), one embedding model and one vector store with the same dimensionality, so images and text compare directly. LangChain and LlamaIndex route text vs image encoding for you. Natively you call different encoders.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0114","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"How do you embed text and images with the same OpenCLIP model, and what do the dimensions show?","a":"<p>Call <code>embed_documents</code> for text and <code>embed_image</code> for images. Both give 1024 dims, so they are directly comparable and storable in ONE vector store.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>clip = OpenCLIPEmbeddings()\ntext_embeddings = clip.embed_documents([&quot;A cat sitting on a couch&quot;])\nimage_embeddings = clip.embed_image([&quot;images/cat1.jpg&quot;])</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0115","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"Why does method 2 (multiple models) need a separate store per model?","a":"<p>Different models give different dimensionalities. Even when dimensions match, embeddings from different training contexts are not meaningfully comparable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0116","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"How do you route queries to the right store in method 2?","a":"<p>Build one store per model (text with <code>HuggingFaceEmbeddings</code> at 768 dims, images with <code>OpenCLIPEmbeddings</code> at 1024 dims) and pick the retriever by data type.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>text_model = HuggingFaceEmbeddings()   # 768 dims\nimage_model = OpenCLIPEmbeddings()     # 1024 dims\ntext_store = FAISS.from_documents(text_docs, embedding=text_model)\nimage_store = FAISS.from_documents(image_docs, embedding=image_model)\ndef multimodal_search(query, query_type=&quot;text&quot;):\n    if query_type == &quot;text&quot;:\n        return text_store.as_retriever().invoke(query)\n    elif query_type == &quot;image&quot;:\n        return image_store.as_retriever().invoke(query)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0117","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"Why would you use method 3 (multiple multimodal models)?","a":"<p>(a) There are too many data types (video, audio, images, tables) for one model. (b) <strong>Re-ranking</strong>: use models trained on different data as a \"second opinion\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0118","card":"q-advanced-rag-030-three-ways-to-do-multimodal-rag","sec":"advanced-rag-s029","q":"What does conceptual routing for method 3 look like?","a":"<p>Route data to different models or stores by type or tag, including an alternate model for re-ranking.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def route_and_embed(data, data_type, tag=None):\n    if data_type == &quot;image_text&quot;:\n        return clip_model.embed(data)\n    elif data_type == &quot;audio_text&quot;:\n        return whisper_clip_model.embed(data)\n    elif tag == &quot;rerank&quot;:\n        return alternate_model.embed(data)  # second model for re-ranking</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0119","card":"q-advanced-rag-031-multimodal-embedding-models","sec":"advanced-rag-s030","q":"How do multimodal embedding models embed several data types, and what are they trained on?","a":"<p>They use internal per-type functions (different preprocessing, different model parts trained on different data). They are usually trained on <strong>pairs</strong>, with image plus text the most common.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0120","card":"q-advanced-rag-031-multimodal-embedding-models","sec":"advanced-rag-s030","q":"What are CLIP, GPT-4o and LLaVA as multimodal models?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Source</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td><strong>CLIP</strong></td><td>OpenAI</td><td>Two encoders (image + text), open source</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>GPT-4o</strong></td><td>OpenAI</td><td>LLM evolved to be multimodal</td></tr>\n      <tr><td><strong>LLaVA</strong></td><td>Open source</td><td>End-to-end transformer: image encoder + Vicuna LLM</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0121","card":"q-advanced-rag-031-multimodal-embedding-models","sec":"advanced-rag-s030","q":"What does CLIP stand for, and why is it the most popular multimodal embedding model?","a":"<p>Contrastive Language-Image Pretraining. It is free, open source and on Hugging Face.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0122","card":"q-advanced-rag-031-multimodal-embedding-models","sec":"advanced-rag-s030","q":"What does \"Contrastive\" mean in CLIP?","a":"<p>It is trained on pairs. Both encoders pull matching pairs close and push unpaired combinations far apart.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0123","card":"q-advanced-rag-031-multimodal-embedding-models","sec":"advanced-rag-s030","q":"What does \"Language-Image\" mean in CLIP?","a":"<p>There are separate text and image encoders, but the vectors land in the <strong>same embedding space</strong> with the <strong>same dimensionality</strong>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0124","card":"q-advanced-rag-031-multimodal-embedding-models","sec":"advanced-rag-s030","q":"What does \"Pretraining\" mean in CLIP?","a":"<p>It was pretrained on 400 million internet image-text pairs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0125","card":"q-advanced-rag-032-demo-embed-and-store","sec":"advanced-rag-s031","q":"How do you embed and store images for the multimodal RAG demo?","a":"<p>It is identical to the image store demo: <code>encode_image</code>, then <code>Document</code>, then <code>FAISS.from_documents</code> with <code>OpenCLIPEmbeddings</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0126","card":"q-advanced-rag-033-demo-query-images-with-text","sec":"advanced-rag-s032","q":"What does <code>resize_base64_image</code> do in the multimodal demo?","a":"<p>It resizes a base64-encoded image (to 128×128 by default) for feeding into OpenAI, and returns it as a base64 PNG.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def resize_base64_image(base64_string: str, size=(128, 128)) -&gt; str:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Resize a base64-encoded image for feeding into OpenAI.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    img_data = base64.b64decode(base64_string)\n    img = Image.open(BytesIO(img_data))\n    img = img.resize(size)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0127","card":"q-advanced-rag-033-demo-query-images-with-text","sec":"advanced-rag-s032","q":"How does <code>split_image_text</code> separate retrieved docs into images and text?","a":"<p>It checks each document's <code>page_content</code> with <code>is_base64</code>. Base64 content goes to the images list and the rest to the text list.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def split_image_text(docs: list) -&gt; dict:\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Split retrieved documents into images and text.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    images = []\n    text = []\n    for doc in docs:\n        if is_base64(doc.page_content):\n            images.append(doc.page_content)\n        else:\n            text.append(doc.page_content)\n    return {&quot;images&quot;: images, &quot;text&quot;: text}</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0128","card":"q-advanced-rag-033-demo-query-images-with-text","sec":"advanced-rag-s032","q":"How does <code>format_multimodal_prompt</code> build the LLM message for text and image context?","a":"<p>It adds a text part for each text context, an <code>image_url</code> part (a base64 PNG data URL of the resized image) for each image, then the question as text, all inside a <code>HumanMessage</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>messages.append({\n    &quot;type&quot;: &quot;image_url&quot;,\n    &quot;image_url&quot;: {&quot;url&quot;: f&quot;data:image/png;base64,{resized}&quot;}\n})\nmessages.append({&quot;type&quot;: &quot;text&quot;, &quot;text&quot;: data.get(&quot;question&quot;, &quot;&quot;)})  # question\nreturn [HumanMessage(content=messages)]</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0129","card":"q-advanced-rag-033-demo-query-images-with-text","sec":"advanced-rag-s032","q":"How is the multimodal chain built in LangChain?","a":"<p>The retriever output goes through <code>RunnableLambda(split_image_text)</code>, then the question and context are formatted into a prompt, then the LLM (<code>gpt-4o-mini</code>), then the string parser.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>llm = ChatOpenAI(model=&quot;gpt-4o-mini&quot;)\nchain = (\n    {\n        &quot;context&quot;: retriever | RunnableLambda(split_image_text),\n        &quot;question&quot;: RunnablePassthrough()\n    }\n    | RunnableLambda(\n        lambda x: format_multimodal_prompt({**x[&quot;context&quot;], &quot;question&quot;: x[&quot;question&quot;]})\n    )\n    | llm\n    | StrOutputParser()\n)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0130","card":"q-advanced-rag-033-demo-query-images-with-text","sec":"advanced-rag-s032","q":"How do you search an image database with text, and how do you check which image matched?","a":"<p>Invoke the chain with a text query. To verify, call the retriever directly and print the image path.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>response = chain.invoke(&quot;Find me a rottweiler&quot;)\nresults = retriever.invoke(&quot;rottweiler&quot;)\nprint(results[0].metadata[&quot;image_path&quot;])  # images/dog_5.jpg</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0131","card":"q-advanced-rag-033-demo-query-images-with-text","sec":"advanced-rag-s032","q":"How does text-to-image retrieval work in the multimodal demo, step by step?","a":"<p>(1) CLIP's text encoder embeds the query. (2) FAISS finds the nearest image embeddings in the same space. (3) Images are resized and base64-formatted. (4) GPT-4o mini describes them. (5) <code>RunnableLambda</code> replaces <code>RunnablePassthrough</code> because a function (splitting images and text) must run, not just pass a string through.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0132","card":"q-advanced-rag-034-11-anomalies-in-embeddings","sec":"advanced-rag-s033","q":"What is the guiding principle behind wrong image results in multimodal RAG, and what are the causes?","a":"<p>Garbage in, garbage out. Text queries sometimes return the wrong image. The causes are bad data quality and the limits of OpenCLIP and its training data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0133","card":"q-advanced-rag-034-11-anomalies-in-embeddings","sec":"advanced-rag-s033","q":"What are the fixes for anomalies in embeddings, and when does each apply?","a":"<div class=\"table-wrap\">\n  <table>\n    <thead><tr><th>Fix</th><th>When</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Fine-tune the embedding model</td><td>You have labeled pairs of your own data</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Improve data quality</td><td>Remove bad or mislabeled images, add diverse examples</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Use rerankers</td><td>A second model re-scores and reorders retrieved results</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0134","card":"q-advanced-rag-034-11-anomalies-in-embeddings","sec":"advanced-rag-s033","q":"What is the debug tip for wrong results in a multimodal RAG chain?","a":"<p>Bypass the LLM and query the retriever directly.</p>\n<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def show_results(query, query_type=&quot;text&quot;):\n    &quot;&quot;&quot;Query the retriever and display results.&quot;&quot;&quot;\n    if query_type == &quot;image&quot;:\n        encoded = encode_image(query)\n        results = retriever.invoke(encoded)\n    else:\n        results = retriever.invoke(query)</code></pre></div>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0135","card":"q-advanced-rag-034-11-anomalies-in-embeddings","sec":"advanced-rag-s033","q":"Which queries gave correct results in the embedding anomalies demo?","a":"<p>\"rottweiler\" returned <code>images/dog_5.jpg</code>. An image query for <code>images/cat_1.jpg</code> returned the same image. \"golden retriever playing with an orange ball\" returned <code>images/dog_2.jpg</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0136","card":"q-advanced-rag-034-11-anomalies-in-embeddings","sec":"advanced-rag-s033","q":"Which queries gave wrong results in the embedding anomalies demo?","a":"<p>\"gray cat with long hair in a field\" should find cat_2 but returned <code>images/dog_5.jpg</code> (a rottweiler). \"golden retriever in a field with a sunny blurred background\" should find dog_4 but returned <code>images/cat_3.jpg</code> (a gray cat on a laptop), and dog_4 was not in the results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0137","card":"q-advanced-rag-034-11-anomalies-in-embeddings","sec":"advanced-rag-s033","q":"Why does OpenCLIP return wrong images for those descriptions?","a":"<p>Its embedding space doesn't separate these specific descriptions well, because of its training data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0138","card":"q-advanced-rag-035-12-resources","sec":"advanced-rag-s034","q":"What does the Advanced RAG course cover chapter by chapter?","a":"<p>Chapter 1: intro and RAG pipeline. Chapter 2: chunking, embeddings, metadata, full text RAG demo. Chapter 3: image search (semantic vs pixel, CNN, ViT, CLIP). Chapter 4: multimodal RAG and anomalies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"advanced-rag-f0139","card":"q-advanced-rag-035-12-resources","sec":"advanced-rag-s034","q":"Which resources does the Advanced RAG course point to for embedding model rankings and an open source CLIP?","a":"<p>The Hugging Face MTEB Leaderboard ranks embedding models. OpenCLIP (github.com/mlfoundations/open_clip) is the open source CLIP implementation.</p>","hand":true}];
